RUN: mkdir -p %t.d
RUN: %PLC --constructors-only -c --fpic %S/includer/includer.st -i "%S/lib/*.st" -o %t.d/a.o
RUN: %PLC -c --single-module --fpic %S/includer/includer.st -i "%S/lib/*.st" -o %t.d/b.o
RUN: %PLC -c --fpic %S/includer/includer.st -i "%S/lib/*.st" -o %t.d/c.o
RUN: %PLC -c --fpic --generate-external-constructors %S/includer/includer.st -i "%S/lib/*.st" -o %t.d/d.o
RUN: cc -shared %t.d/a.o %t.d/b.o -o %t.d/libab.so
RUN: cc -shared %t.d/a.o %t.d/c.o -o %t.d/libac.so
RUN: cc -shared %t.d/a.o %t.d/d.o -o %t.d/libad.so
RUN: nm -D --defined-only %t.d/libab.so %t.d/libac.so %t.d/libad.so | not grep -E ' (__I[A-Z]|_*itable_I)'

RUN: sed -E 's/^(TYPE|INTERFACE|FUNCTION_BLOCK|FUNCTION) /{external} \1 /' %S/lib/*.st > %t.d/external.st
RUN: %PLC -c --single-module --fpic %S/includer/includer.st %t.d/external.st -o %t.d/eb.o
RUN: %PLC -c --fpic %S/includer/includer.st %t.d/external.st -o %t.d/ec.o
RUN: cc -shared %t.d/eb.o -o %t.d/libeb.so
RUN: cc -shared %t.d/ec.o -o %t.d/libec.so
RUN: nm -D --defined-only %t.d/libeb.so %t.d/libec.so | not grep -E ' (__I[A-Z]|_*itable_I)'

Each object built from includer/ includes the library in lib/ but does not
declare its interfaces, so none of them may define the constructors of the
interface parameter types or the itables of the interfaces. The
constructors-only object is linked into one shared library with each of the
other objects, without the library object, and no shared library may export
these symbols, because an exported symbol replaces the definition of the
library that declares the interface.

The second group compiles the same declarations as source, with each one marked
{external}. An {external} declaration is a declaration of another unit, as with
-i, so the result must be the same. Each object is linked into its own shared
library. --constructors-only and --generate-external-constructors are not part
of this group, because they define the constructors of {external} declarations
on purpose.
